{{/*
  offer-file-as-bead — reusable "instead of executing now, file this as a
  beads issue (or epic)" option snippet.

  The consuming prompt has just presented a plan / prioritized change list /
  set of proposed actions and is at the "wait for user approval" step. This
  fragment provides:

    1. The prose describing the option (what it means, when it's chosen).
    2. The exact gating condition to include in a `mitto_ui_options` list.
    3. Post-selection guidance — single-issue vs epic, safety rails on
       `bd close` / `bd update --claim`.

  It intentionally does NOT emit the whole `mitto_ui_options` call — the
  caller keeps ownership of the surrounding menu so it can add its own
  approval buttons ("Approve all", "Approve selected", "Cancel", etc.).

  The fragment is emitted only when `bd` and `.beads` are both present.
  When neither is, the whole block is empty so the caller can concatenate
  its own menu unconditionally.

  Consumers: docs/propose-a-plan, code/refactor, code/optimize,
  code/cleanup-code, code/simplify, docs/implement-spec.

  Usage — parameter-less; render against the ambient
  *PromptEnabledContext so `.Session` and the FuncMap resolve:

      {{ template "_shared/offer-file-as-bead" . }}

  Callers embed the resulting text right next to their approval menu. The
  option label used inside the caller's `options: [...]` list is:

      {{`{{- if and (CommandExists "bd") (DirExists ".beads") }}`}}
      {label: "File this as a beads issue (or epic) instead"},
      {{`{{- end }}`}}
*/}}
{{- if and (CommandExists "bd") (DirExists ".beads") }}
**Optional: file this as a beads issue (or epic) instead of executing now.**

Beads (`bd`) is configured in this workspace, so the proposal above can be
tracked as a durable issue rather than executed immediately. When adding your
approval menu, include this option **gated on `bd` availability**:

```
{{`{{- if and (CommandExists "bd") (DirExists ".beads") }}`}}
{label: "File this as a beads issue (or epic) instead"},
{{`{{- end }}`}}
```

If the user picks it, decide between a single issue and an epic based on the
proposal's scope:

- **Single issue** when the proposal is one tightly-scoped change — roughly
  ≲ 3 concrete actions with a shared goal — that can be captured in one
  `bd create` call.
- **Epic (with one child issue per proposal item)** when the proposal spans
  multiple independent milestones, subsystems, or would take more than a
  working day. Create the parent epic first with `bd create --type epic`,
  then one child issue per item under it via `bd create --parent <epic-id>`.

Safety rails when filing:

- Do **NOT** run `bd close` — issue closure is always the user's decision.
- Do **NOT** claim the issue (`bd update <id> --claim`) unless the user
  also asked to start executing it right away.
- Preserve enough of the proposal's context (priorities, effort estimates,
  affected files) in the issue body that someone picking it up later can
  act on it without replaying this conversation.
{{- end }}
